WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.493
Your heat exchanger was designed clean.

00:00:02.793 --> 00:00:04.733
Six months later, it's struggling.

00:00:04.733 --> 00:00:06.616
Nobody touched it. What happened?

00:00:07.116 --> 00:00:09.769
Same exchanger. Same flows.

00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:12.815
Hot in at 90, cold in at 10 °C.

00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:15.712
Clean, the cold stream left at 63.

00:00:15.712 --> 00:00:17.240
Now it leaves at 60.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.889
What changed is a layer inside the tubes,

00:00:20.889 --> 00:00:24.345
0.08 mm thick. Thinner than a sheet of paper.

00:00:24.645 --> 00:00:26.045
It doesn't block the flow.

00:00:26.045 --> 00:00:27.768
It sits in the path of the heat.

00:00:28.268 --> 00:00:30.412
Resistances in series simply add.

00:00:30.712 --> 00:00:34.591
Clean, the resistance to heat flow is

00:00:34.591 --> 00:00:38.680
0.0020 m²·K/W. The deposit adds 0.0004.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:44.415
So U falls from 500 to about 417 W/m²·K.

00:00:44.915 --> 00:00:47.016
That sliver resists heat like

00:00:47.016 --> 00:00:49.336
about 6.4 mm of stainless steel.

00:00:49.836 --> 00:00:52.728
Lower U means fewer transfer units:

00:00:52.728 --> 00:00:54.380
2 becomes about 1.7.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.876
Effectiveness slips from 0.67 to 0.63,

00:00:57.876 --> 00:01:01.493
and the exchanger moves about 6% less heat.

00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:04.833
6% is easy to miss. Flow is normal.

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:07.476
The only clue is a drifting thermometer.

00:01:07.976 --> 00:01:10.165
Fouling is a resistance in series.

00:01:10.165 --> 00:01:12.869
Watch the temperatures, not just the flow.

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:16.154
These numbers are an illustrative worked example.

00:01:16.654 --> 00:01:19.075
Next: why are there plates inside the shell?
